SECTION 1: WHY TESTING?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
• Prevents regressions
• Safe refactoring
• Improves code quality
• Required for CI/CD pipelines
• Interviewers expect you to know testing depth at SDE-2

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 2: TYPES OF TESTS IN SPRING BOOT
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1️⃣ Unit Test
   - Tests ONE class
   - Dependencies are mocked
   - Fast
   - Uses Mockito + JUnit
   - No Spring context

2️⃣ Slice Test
   - Tests ONE LAYER
   - Examples:
     @WebMvcTest  → Controller layer
     @DataJpaTest → Repository layer

3️⃣ Integration Test
   - Full application context
   - Uses real beans
   - Slower
   - Uses @SpringBootTest

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 3: JUNIT 5 BASICS (MUST KNOW)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Annotations:
• @Test          → Marks test method
• @BeforeEach   → Runs before every test
• @AfterEach
• @BeforeAll    → Runs once (static)
• @AfterAll
• @DisplayName  → Readable test name

Assertions:
• assertEquals(expected, actual)
• assertNotNull(obj)
• assertTrue(condition)
• assertThrows(Exception.class, () -> method())
• assertAll(() -> {}, () -> {})

INTERVIEW LINE:
"JUnit provides test lifecycle management and assertions."

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 4: MOCKITO – MOST IMPORTANT PART
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why Mockito?
• To isolate business logic
• To mock dependencies
• To avoid DB, network, external calls

Core Annotations:
• @Mock
    - Creates fake object
    - No real method execution

• @InjectMocks
    - Creates real object
    - Injects mocks into it

• @Spy
    - Partial mock (real + fake)

Enable Mockito:
• @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 5: STUBBING IN MOCKITO
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Stubbing = defining mock behavior

Examples:
• when(repo.findById(1)).thenReturn(data);
• when(service.call()).thenThrow(Exception.class);
• doNothing().when(repo).save(any());

IMPORTANT:
• use doReturn() for:
  - spies
  - final methods
  - void methods

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 6: VERIFYING INTERACTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
verify(repo).save(100);
verify(repo, times(1)).save(any());
verify(repo, never()).delete(any());

INTERVIEW LINE:
"verify() ensures interaction happened, not just output."

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 7: ARGUMENT CAPTOR (INTERVIEW FAVORITE ⭐)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Used when:
• You want to capture values passed to a mock

Example:
ArgumentCaptor<Integer> captor =
    ArgumentCaptor.forClass(Integer.class);

verify(repo).save(captor.capture());
assertEquals(100, captor.getValue());

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 8: EXCEPTION TESTING
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
How to test exception?

assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> {
    service.pay(-1);
});

INTERVIEW LINE:
"I use assertThrows to validate error scenarios."

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 9: SPRING BOOT TEST ANNOTATIONS (HIGHLY ASKED)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@SpringBootTest
• Loads FULL application context
• Slow
• Used for integration tests

@WebMvcTest(Controller.class)
• Loads only MVC components
• Used for controller tests
• Uses MockMvc
• Dependencies mocked using @MockBean

@DataJpaTest
• Tests repository layer
• Uses in-memory DB (H2)
• Fast
• Rolls back after each test

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 10: @Mock vs @MockBean (VERY COMMON)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@Mock
• From Mockito
• No Spring context
• Used in unit tests

@MockBean
• From Spring Boot
• Replaces bean in Spring context
• Used in controller/integration tests

ONE-LINER ANSWER:
"@Mock is for unit tests, @MockBean is for Spring context tests."

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 11: CONTROLLER TESTING (MockMvc)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MockMvc:
• Tests controller without starting server
• Simulates HTTP requests

Used with:
@WebMvcTest

Example flow:
• mock service
• perform request
• expect status & response

INTERVIEW LINE:
"MockMvc tests REST APIs without deploying application."

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 12: REPOSITORY TESTING
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@DataJpaTest
• Uses real repository
• Uses embedded DB
• No service or controller layer

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 13: UNIT VS INTEGRATION TEST
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Unit Test:
• Fast
• Mocked dependencies
• Business logic focus

Integration Test:
• Slow
• Real beans
• Real DB / messaging

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 14: COMMON INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Q: Why Mockito?
A: To isolate class under test.

Q: Why @WebMvcTest?
A: To test controller layer only.

Q: How do you test exceptions?
A: assertThrows()

Q: How to test DB?
A: @DataJpaTest

Q: How to test full flow?
A: @SpringBootTest

